  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Partial fit. If the addressed bytes are the whole envelope, hashing binds pcid and payload, so Bob can recover interpretation without a filename and unknown pcids fail explicitly. But the scenario's CID codec identity alone test is only partly met: the outer codec does not by itself distinguish raw versus pointer semantics, which are delegated to the pcid-named inner protocol. Unsigned v0 also weakens provenance and audit strength.",
    "strengths": [
      "If the whole envelope is the addressed object, the hash binds both pcid and payload.",
      "Opaque-payload and no-speculative-parse rules create a very clear dispatch boundary.",
      "Unknown-pcid handling preserves exact bytes and works under sparse knowledge."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "CID codec identity alone still does not tell Bob whether bytes are raw or pointer; he needs the envelope and handler.",
      "The envelope delegates raw-versus-pointer typing to the inner protocol rather than defining CAS object binding itself.",
      "Unsigned v0 leaves provenance, authorship, and accountability outside the envelope."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If systems hash or exchange only inner payload bytes, the type-binding benefit disappears.",
      "Long-term readability depends on durable availability of pcid specs and handlers.",
      "Without a native signature, envelopes can be replayed or misattributed unless outer layers add accountability."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What CID codec should identify the envelope bytes so Bob knows he is looking at a grid envelope rather than arbitrary bytes?",
      "Should raw chunks and pointer objects map to distinct pcids, or should a deeper inner type system carry that distinction?",
      "How will pcid meanings be frozen and rediscovered over decades without a central registry?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
